# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A block rule runs only where a block starts"

# TiddlyWiki opens a block at a blank line and nowhere else. A fence written straight under a
# paragraph line does not start a block: the paragraph carries on, and the parser reads the
# backticks as an unterminated inline code delimiter — it says so itself, with an
# `unterminated-codeinline` diagnostic, and builds no code block.
#
# So a fence gets an embedded language only after a blank line. Colouring the first form as a
# code block would tell an author their JavaScript runs where the wiki renders backticks in a
# paragraph. The blank line cures it, and the absent colour shows it missing.

! Straight under a paragraph line, no block starts

A paragraph line.
```js
#^^^^^ meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#^^^^^ - meta.codeblock.tiddlywiki5
#^^^^^ - source.js
var a = 1;
#^^^^^^^^^^ - source.js
```

! After a blank line, the block starts and the language embeds

A paragraph line.

```js
#^^^^^ markup.fenced_code.block.tiddlywiki5 meta.codeblock.tiddlywiki5
var a = 1;
#^^^ source.js
```
